MODERN_DISCLOSURE='We were unable to establish a faithful project-scale experimental comparison with the examined public implementations under the fixed reproduction protocol.'
TITLE='Source-Anchored LMMSE Channel Estimation with Cross-Pilot Fitting and Low-Rank Corrections'
SAFE_MISSING='Within the reviewed nearest-neighbor set, we did not identify the complete combination of a frozen full Source empirical LMMSE estimator, prepared statistical difference directions, unrestricted real coordinates fitted by four-fold cross-pilot prediction over a current-inclusive five-frame window, and rank-four compression confined to the deployed corrections.'
CONSERVATIVE=[
 'We study pilot-only adaptation of a frozen empirical LMMSE channel estimator using prepared statistical correction directions for MIMO-OFDM.',
 'We formulate a four-fold cross-pilot prediction objective over a current-inclusive five-frame window and fit two unrestricted real coordinates through a small normal-equation solve.',
 'We retain the full Source estimator and apply rank-four compression to the deployed correction operators, using the same pilot-fitted coordinates as the dense construction.',
 'We evaluate the frozen receiver under the registered formal and independent sealed protocols, and report development sensitivity studies and post-hoc receiver diagnostics with their distinct evidence roles.']
MODERATE=[
 'We develop a Source-anchored LMMSE adaptation interface in which online pilots determine coordinates of prepared profile and mobility correction operators.',
 'We combine geometry-defined cross-pilot prediction with causal multi-frame coordinate fitting, avoiding online target clean CSI, target covariance, and condition labels.',
 'We compress only the correction operators while preserving the full Source estimator, and examine the resulting dense-to-compressed performance difference under frozen validation protocols.',
 'We establish Source-relative evidence within the tested channel conditions and report the limits of unseen-profile validation, offline statistical budgets, pilot geometry, and modern-baseline comparability.']

